The finite-horizon interval is descriptive for the longer-horizon sweep. Testbed's upper threshold is approximately $1.01$ reward units per nat, placing nat-canonical $w=1$ inside and implemented $w=1$ outside. Yet at $H=4$ the two implementations have identical reward, success and usage (\texttt{results\_\allowbreak nat\_\allowbreak canonical\_\allowbreak check.csv}) and both over-observe relative to $w^*_{\mathrm{ret}}\in[0.01,0.5]$. On Tiger and Diagnosis, $w=1$ belongs to the observed reward-maximizing range. Tileworld contradicts that extrapolation: its unique sampled optimum is $w=20$ in bit units ($28.9$ per nat), above the heuristic upper threshold $7.91$, and $w=1$ is Pareto-dominated.

Figure~\ref{fig:state_preservation} isolates the timing distinction behind Example~\ref{ex:destructive}.

\begin{figure}[tbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_state_preservation.pdf}
\caption{An observation can describe a state that no longer exists. These schematic timelines use a perfect sensor to contrast state-preserving sensing with the destructive test of Example~\ref{ex:destructive}. The sensor reports the pre-action state in both cases. Under preservation that report identifies the state used by the next decision. Under destruction both possible initial states become $0$, so the correct post-action belief is $(1,0)$ for either report. Treating a report of $1$ as the current state gives an obsolete posterior. The example limits the state-preserving reduction, not transition-aware EFE.}
\label{fig:state_preservation}
\Description{Paired state timelines. State-preserving sensing takes state 0 through report 0 to state 0, and state 1 through report 1 to state 1. Destructive sensing emits the same reports but takes both initial states to state 0. The report-1 branch ends in state 0 after destruction, making the old-state report unsuitable as the current state.}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Agent Specifications and Weight Selection}
\label{app:theory_agents}

Planning+IG uses EFE's tree search at the same horizon, adding $wI_a(b)$ to observation values. It represents explicit information rewards in POMDP-IR and $\rho$-POMDPs \citep{spaan2015,araya2010}. \citet{satsangi2018} prove equivalence of those formulations for piecewise-linear convex belief rewards, a result we do not invoke for the concave expected-IG reward here. Proposition~\ref{prop:equivalence} identifies EFE with Planning+IG at $w{=}1$, so within this shared implementation tuned-weight comparisons isolate the weight. The \texttt{pymdp} check \citep{heins2022} is qualitative consistency evidence, not certified numerical equivalence (Appendix~\ref{app:pymdp}).

Epistemic-only sets $\mathcal{G}(\text{commit})=0$, comparing information gain with sensing cost without reward-aware stopping. When it commits, it chooses the belief-argmax state. No observation's information gain clears its cost on Tiger, Diagnosis, Bandit, or Tileworld, so it commits from the prior at chance accuracy. Testbed's cheaper observation is the exception, where the ablation matches Planning on observations and success (Appendices~\ref{app:full_tables} and~\ref{app:testbed}).

Myopic ($\rho{=}0,H{=}1$) can observe because its lookahead includes one observation followed by a commit, capturing instrumental value of information without an epistemic bonus. Greedy samples unchecked rocks in RockSample and diagnoses from the prior without tests in Inspection. The IDS adaptation minimizes squared expected regret divided by information about the optimal commit \citep{russo2014}, with its fidelity limits and results in Appendix~\ref{app:ids}. Posterior-vote draws $100$ states from the belief and votes over their optimal actions. It approaches a deterministic most-likely-state rule, unlike Thompson sampling, which acts optimally for one posterior draw and retains probability matching. The CSV label \texttt{ThompsonSamplingAgent} denotes posterior-vote.

Info Gain and Planning+IG are success-tuned within their own classes over $\{0.1,0.5,1,2,5,10,20,50,100\}$. Each candidate uses $200$ episodes, or $100$ on the more expensive Tileworld, on the common tuning stream $7$, reset for every candidate. Ties select the smallest weight (\texttt{tune\_\allowbreak info\_\allowbreak gain\_\allowbreak weight}, \texttt{experiments/\allowbreak run\_\allowbreak experiment.py}). The selected $w^*_{\mathrm{succ}}$ therefore carries single-stream sampling noise. This stream is disjoint from the five evaluation seeds. Although seed $7$ also appears in Section~\ref{sec:budget_frontier}'s held-out set, that study evaluates only the calibrated family, never a tuned baseline. Section~\ref{sec:pareto}'s separate eleven-weight sweep selects reward-maximizing $w^*_{\mathrm{ret}}$. Neither tuning quantity is the budget crossing $w^*(B)$ or Proposition~\ref{prop:nearopt}'s thresholds, despite the shared star notation. Section~\ref{sec:budget} instead estimates $w^*(B)$ by adjacent grid weights whose usage crosses the target.

